  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Monday...\n\nSmoke Impacts: Wildfire smoke will continue to impact our region \nfor the foreseeable future, keeping skies quite hazy, with reduced\nsurface visibilities and poor air quality at times.\n\nSatellite imagery shows dry zonal flow over the region with a weak\nshortwave (responsible for the morning ACCAS field) about to exit\neastern MT. Pwats continue to increase and as of 17z are analyzed\nto be an inch east of Rosebud County. As was the case yesterday,\nthere is potential for a t-storm or two over our far east late\nthis afternoon and early evening with the question being whether\nor not a mid level cap can be overcome. Low levels show an area of\nconvergence in far southeast MT which may provide a forcing\nmechanism, but otherwise we will be under large scale descent\nbehind the passing wave. Most CAMs show no development in\nsoutheast MT but will keep isolated TS mentioned per the\nenvironment. If a storm does develop it could be on the strong\nside, with ample shear and 1000+ j/kg of MLCape. Early this\nmorning there was an area of stratus over Carter County, and there\nseems to be a risk of fog late tonight into early Tuesday in our \nfar east, from roughly 09z-15z. Low level easterly winds will keep\nboundary layer moisture elevated in this area.\n\nWarm & dry conditions continue Tuesday, with elevated RHs in the\neast and mins in the teens from Rosebud County westward. Once\nagain, the environment may favor a strong t-storm or two in our\nfar east by late in the day. Otherwise it will be dry with temps\nin the upper 80s and lower 90s. Winds won't be strong but N-NE\nwinds in the afternoon will be a bit gustier than today, with\ngusts ~20 mph. This will keep fire weather conditions elevated.\n\nThe entire forecast area begins to turn cooler and more humid on\nWednesday, as heights fall in response to a trof dropping out of\nsouthwest Canada, low level easterly winds moisten the boundary\nlayer further west, and mid/upper level moisture is increased per\nmonsoonal flow. All told, shower and t-storm potential increases \nsignificantly by Wednesday afternoon and evening. SPC has the far \nsoutheast corner of MT highlighted for a marginal risk of severe \nt-storms. Heavy rain is the larger risk, especially in the east on\nWednesday.\n\nThursday into Friday looks to be the time when pwats are highest,\nup to 1.25\" as far west as Billings per ensembles. There is a\nshortwave emerging from the southwest, providing ascent for what \nshould be a period of widespread showers and t-storms capable of \nproducing heavy rain. WPC has our area highlighted with a marginal\nto slight risk of heavy rain. Local burn scars will be at an \nelevated risk of seeing flash flooding. Regarding fire weather, \nthe onset of increased lightning activity may be problematic for \npossible new fire starts, as almost our entire forecast area has \nnot seen measurable rain yet in August, but the much wetter air \nmass should also keep potential fire growth in check. This will be\nsomething to monitor.\n\nHeights rise this weekend as another Pacific low settles off the \ncoast, and temps will climb again. Pwats will drop some but remain\nelevated, and additional shortwaves emerging from the southwest \nflow aloft will continue the daily chance of t-storms Saturday \nthrough Monday. \n\nLook for temperatures to cool to the 80s Wednesday then 70s\nThursday and Friday. The next warming trend begins Saturday and we\nshould see 80s to lower 90s return by early next week.\n\nJKL",
